Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday, appealing for international aid as Cyclone Ditwah’s torrential rains and floods resulted in 132 fatalities and left 176 individuals unaccounted for. According to the Disaster Management Centre (DMC), as reported by AFP, the severe weather system demolished over 15,000 residences and compelled 78,000 individuals to seek refuge in government-run temporary shelters.

DMC Director-General Sampath Kotuwegoda stated, “We have 132 confirmed dead and another 176 missing.” He further mentioned that relief efforts have been bolstered with forces from the army, navy, and air force. Despite the cyclone moving north toward India on Saturday, new landslides occurred in Kandy, 115 km (70 miles) east of Colombo, inundating several sections of the primary access road. Concurrently, worsening floods in low-lying regions led authorities to mandate evacuations for residents along the Kelani River, which empties into the Indian Ocean from Colombo.

The government appealed for global assistance and encouraged Sri Lankans abroad to donate funds to support nearly half a million people impacted by the calamity. India promptly responded, dispatching relief supplies and essential Humanitarian Assistance and Disaster Relief (HADR) equipment. An Indian warship docked in Colombo for a goodwill visit also contributed its provisions to support victims.

Prime Minister Narendra Modi expressed his sympathy to the people of Sri Lanka in a post on X, lamenting the lives lost to the cyclone and reaffirming India’s commitment to aiding its “closest maritime neighbour.” He emphasized that India had swiftly dispatched assistance as part of the initiative.
