The Deputy Minister of Agriculture and Rural Development, Phung Duc Tien, remarked that the nation might still face its 16th storm of the year as 2025 draws to a close. Hoang Duc Cuong, Deputy Director of the Hydrometeorological Department, offered an overview of the year's natural disaster and hydrometeorological developments, highlighting that 2025 has experienced highly atypical weather patterns and disasters, resulting in significant damage across the country.

Notably, the Bach Ma station in Thua Thien Hue recorded an extraordinary 1,739 mm of rainfall within a single day in October. In the East Sea, the frequency of storms and tropical depressions has hit its peak since Vietnam initiated record-keeping in 1961. The 2025 storm season was marked by a host of uncommon and severe events. Storm No. 1 emerged in the East Sea in June, an occurrence not witnessed in over four decades.

The floods of 2025 were notably extensive and intense. Many rivers experienced peak water levels that exceeded past historical highs. For the first time in a single year, 20 rivers recorded either extreme or historic flooding, including areas formerly part of Phu Yen province. Deputy Director Hoang Duc Cuong observed that the array of atypical occurrences in Vietnam and globally during 2025 indicates a shift in conventional flood patterns.

The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) is in the process of assessing this figure to potentially acknowledge it as one of the world's highest recorded 24-hour rainfall totals. The East Sea experienced an unprecedented surge in storm and tropical depression occurrences, marking the highest activity level recorded since Vietnam's documentation began in 1961.

During late November, a tropical depression transitioned from the Indian Ocean to the northwestern Pacific, marking an unprecedented path in the annals of meteorological records. The floods of 2025 were notably extensive and intense, with river levels reaching unprecedented heights across numerous areas. These floods stretched from the Northern and Central territories to the Central Highlands, further impacting the lower Mekong Delta.

Significantly, the historic flood in eastern Dak Lak, which was once a section of Phu Yen province, surpassed the 1993 flood peak levels on the Ba River basin. The pattern of intensifying weather extremes is becoming more evident, with notable variations in storms, precipitation, and flooding directly impacted by climate change.
