A remarkably intense storm for the area struck in November, churning over the South China Sea as it neared Vietnam. Its winds were consistently blowing at speeds of approximately 114 mph, with gusts potentially climbing to 137 mph.

Vietnam's central provinces are currently experiencing severe flooding due to record-breaking rains. Kalmaegi is expected to unleash over 24 inches of rain in certain regions. Officials cautioned that as much as 4 inches of rain might flood regions at lower elevations.

In coastal cities like Danang, waves up to 10ft high battered the coast. Robust winds have toppled trees in Dak Lak province. Numerous residences in Quy Nhon, another coastal city, experienced prolonged power outages. High tides were anticipated on the Saigon River. Ho Chi Minh City, the nation's economic center, confronts an increased threat of significant flooding.

Spanning the central regions of the Philippines, Kalmaegi claimed the lives of at least 114 individuals and left 127 unaccounted for, marking the most devastating natural catastrophe to strike the nation this year. After unleashing devastation and loss of life in the Philippines, with the central province of Cebu bearing the brunt, the tropical cyclone exited the archipelago on Wednesday, heading into the South China Sea.

In the Cebu town of Liloan, the bodies of her husband and three children, who perished when their roof caved in, were kept in preparation for a wake. On Thursday, Krizza Espra visited the mortuary, explaining that four members of her family — her mother and aunt among them — are still unaccounted for. “I hope someone can help speed up the search,” she said.

According to the Office of Civil Defence, the typhoon forced the displacement of over 560,000 villagers in the Philippines, with nearly 450,000 of them being relocated to emergency shelters.

With President Ferdinand Marcos Jr.'s declaration of a "state of calamity" on Thursday, the government is enabled to expedite the release of emergency funds and curb both food hoarding and excessive pricing.

Officials in charge of disaster response have cautioned that an approaching tropical cyclone from the Pacific may intensify into a super typhoon, potentially striking the northern Philippines at the start of next week.

Six fatalities linked to Kalmaegi occurred when a Philippine military helicopter met a tragic end in the southern province of Agusan del Sur on Tuesday. According to the military, the crew was en route to deliver humanitarian aid to areas heavily impacted by the typhoon.

Kalmaegi unleashed a deluge equivalent to one-and-a-half months of precipitation in merely one day on Tuesday across metropolitan Cebu, according to state forecaster Benison Estareja. Heavy rains triggered flash floods, swelling a river along with other waterways in Cebu city and its neighboring towns. Provincial officials reported that the ensuing floodwaters submerged residential areas, compelling inhabitants to seek refuge on their rooftops, where they frantically called for help as the water levels rapidly increased.

Torrential floodwaters engulfed or carried off numerous vehicles in Cebu's residential areas, with dramatic footage captured by residents who were marooned on rooftops. A minimum of 71 fatalities occurred in Cebu, primarily attributed to drowning incidents.

Authorities also noted that an additional 62 individuals were unaccounted for in the central province of Negros Occidental, close to Cebu.

"We took all possible measures for the typhoon, yet, as you know, unforeseen events like flash floods can still occur," Governor Pamela Baricuatro conveyed to The Associated Press over the phone. Ms. Baricuatro stated that the storm's impacts might have been aggravated by quarrying activities that led to river blockage and overflow, combined with inadequate flood control measures in Cebu province.

A scandal surrounding inadequate or entirely absent flood control projects throughout the Philippines has ignited public indignation and led to street demonstrations in recent months.

Additionally, Cebu continues to mend from the aftermath of a 6.9 magnitude earthquake that struck on September 30, resulting in at least 79 fatalities and the displacement of numerous individuals.
