Colombo's northern region and areas adjacent to the Kelani River are experiencing severe flooding. "Intense rainfall upstream is causing flooding in the low-lying regions adjacent to the Kelani River banks," stated a Disaster Management Centre (DMC) official. Dozens of families remain stranded in their homes due to rising floodwaters in Colombo.

Approximately 200,000 individuals have been rescued and relocated to shelters. Over 20,000 homes have been devastated by the severe weather system, displacing 122,000 individuals who now reside in government-operated temporary shelters. An additional 833,000 people require humanitarian assistance.

Personnel from the army, navy, and air force have been dispatched to work in conjunction with civilian workers and volunteers in relief efforts. Authorities confirmed power and communication services will resume within three days, though approximately one-third of the nation still lacks electricity and access to running water due to downed power lines and flooded water treatment plants. Internet services have also faced interruptions.

President Anura Kumara Dissanayake announced a state of emergency on Saturday and sought assistance from the international community. India promptly dispatched relief supplies along with two helicopters and their crews for rescue operations. Japan announced plans to dispatch a team to evaluate urgent requirements and committed to providing additional support.

Although rainfall has diminished throughout the island, numerous roads in the hardest-hit central province remain impassable. This cyclone represents the most lethal natural catastrophe to strike Sri Lanka since 2017, when floods and landslides resulted in over 200 fatalities and displaced hundreds of thousands. The most severe flooding since the beginning of the 21st century occurred in June 2003, claiming 254 lives.
