data.fused_content:
The Labor government is set to allocate $200 million towards 96 projects across the nation, aimed at bolstering community resilience against severe weather conditions. This includes initiatives on Palm Island, located off the coast of Queensland, which faces increased threats from tropical cyclones and intense storms. Here, an 800-person capacity cyclone shelter and evacuation center are planned for construction.

From the total $200 million allocated by the federal government, a significant portion exceeding 50% is earmarked for infrastructure initiatives with immediate construction initiation. Approved projects will enable various natural disaster relief initiatives, including establishing recovery centers, flood protection infrastructure, reducing the dangers posed by bushfires, and implementing sophisticated alert systems for natural threats.

Additional initiatives encompass a $1.6 million infrastructure undertaking aimed at safeguarding Townsvale Estate in South Australia, a $14.1 million investment for enhancing stormwater infrastructure in Narrandera in Central NSW, and $895,000 to enhance the capacity of Karajarri Rangers, focusing on reducing severe wildfire threats in northwest WA.

This declaration marks the third round of financial support from the $1 billion Disaster Ready Fund (DRF), a governmental program dedicated to enhancing disaster resilience and mitigating risks. In earlier rounds, the DRF allocated roughly $400 million in federal funds to over 350 projects. Almost 50% of these projects are currently in the pre-construction or construction-delivery stages, with 43 projects already approaching completion or finalized.

"Australians are well-acquainted with the annual onslaught of floods, bushfires, and cyclones, which is precisely why the Albanese government initiated the Disaster Ready Fund, allocating up to $1 billion over the span of five years," stated Kristy McBain, the emergency management minister. "From constructing cyclone shelters on Palm Island to strategizing flood mitigation in Launceston, the third round will advantage every one of the 538 local government areas spanning all states and territories. Importantly, we reached $600 million in total investment through the DRF – and I am grateful to numerous project backers for matching our funding."
====ALIGNMENT====
[
