In the first nine months of 2025, extreme weather events in India were linked to as many as 4,064 fatalities, according to a joint report by the Delhi-based think tank Centre for Science and Environment (CSE) and Down To Earth (DTE). The analysis highlighted a 48 percent rise in such deaths over the past four years.

Madhya Pradesh recorded the highest number of fatalities, while Maharashtra suffered the most significant impact in terms of crop damage, with 8.4 million hectares affected. The total crop damage reached an astonishing 9.47 million hectares, marking a fourfold increase over four years.

Throughout this period, the nation experienced some form of extreme weather conditions—including heat and cold waves, lightning, storms, heavy rainfall, floods, and landslides—on 99 percent of the days, resulting in these recorded losses. A minimum of 30 states and Union Territories faced persistent extreme weather conditions from February through September 2025, with Himachal Pradesh witnessing the highest number of events. Since 2022, at least 18 states or Union territories have documented their peak count of extreme weather days.

Among the 4,064 fatalities recorded in 2025, 3,007 occurred during the monsoon season, which continues to be the most destructive period.

The Climate India 2025 report, an annual publication unveiled on November 19, 2025, during a virtual seminar hosted by Sunita Narain, director general of CSE and editor of DTE, draws upon extensive observation data. Narain remarked, "The country no longer needs to count just the disasters. What we need to understand is the scale—the scale of mitigation that Belém is talking about, the scale on which the whole world has to come together." She added, "Given the intensity and frequency of extreme weather events, it is also about what we need to do, keeping in mind that there will be more and more such disasters."
