Unpredictable weather patterns have triggered an unusual occurrence in Korea, decimating apple harvests. As global temperatures climb and rainfall intensifies, the fruit is fighting for survival.

For farmers who have been growing apples in Korea's South Jeolla Province, this was devastating to their harvests and shocking. "If I had 100 apples last year, about 80 split open," apple farmer Yoon Young-hwan told The Korea Times. "This year, I tried everything I could to prevent it, but still about 60% cracked."

Farmers refer to this phenomenon as 'yeolgwa'. The rapid uptake of water is something the apple's skin fails to accommodate swiftly, resulting in the fruit splitting open. "When I started farming apples in 1972, this concept didn't even exist," noted Yoon.

Apples thrive in cooler climates, ideally when temperatures range from 45 to 52 degrees Fahrenheit. As nations persist in utilizing non-renewable energy, the emission of harmful gases exacerbates planetary warming and sets off extreme weather events that threaten agricultural stability.

What's being done to address Korea's apple industry hardships? Transitioning to sub-tropical fruits for Korean apple growers, while the nation imports apples, is both expensive and labor-intensive. "It's frustrating to hear about importing apples when we can't even grow them properly," Yoon told The Korea Times. "The entire apple industry could face collapse."
