The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) of the United Nations advocates for broadening global monitoring and forecasting systems to warn individuals in developing countries about severe weather conditions. WMO Secretary-General Celeste Saulo emphasized during her address at the UN's yearly conference in Geneva that "early warning means early action," asserting that the objective is not only to warn the world but to empower it.

A shift in the global climate has led to more frequent and severe occurrences of extreme weather events, such as hurricanes, flooding, wildfires, and glacier collapses. The UN's meteorological agency has noted that over the past five decades, climate-related incidents have resulted in the deaths of more than 2 million individuals. Almost 50% of countries face heightened exposure to these phenomena, with developing nations being especially susceptible due to inadequate alert systems and defenses.

The WMO expressed worry over insufficient safeguards and emphasized that implementing early warning systems could avert most fatalities attributed to such conditions. These systems provide individuals with additional time for evacuation and readiness, allowing them to strengthen their surroundings or evacuate safely. Broader implementation has the potential to preserve lives, and with the establishment of operational systems in more countries, the preservation of lives will increase.

While advancements have been achieved and more nations are adopting early warning systems, the WMO acknowledged that significant efforts are still needed. Half of the countries utilizing these systems are engaging only with their fundamental features, while 16% operate below even basic levels. The WMO is focused on raising awareness about the underuse of such systems and strives to enhance their recognition and implementation globally.
