A massive illegal waste dump, believed to be the largest case of fly-tipping in the UK, has been discovered on a floodplain adjacent to the A34 and the River Cherwell near Kidlington in Oxfordshire. The enormous pile spans 500 feet in length, reaches a depth of 20 feet, and weighs several hundred tonnes.

The waste, which includes materials such as plastic, foam, and wood, was deposited all at once and discovered last month by horrified local anglers. An MP has labeled the site "threatening an environmental disaster."

Charity Friends of the Thames stated, "This is an environmental catastrophe unfolding in plain sight. With each passing day, the likelihood of toxic run-off seeping into the river system grows, endangering wildlife and compromising the health of the whole catchment area."

The Environment Agency has confirmed an investigation is underway. A spokesperson said, "Expert officers are currently probing the waste disposal incident. We share the public’s anger about incidents like this, which is why we take action against those responsible for waste crime." The agency urges anyone with information to contact its 24-hour incident hotline.

Due to the considerable height of the waste, portions have collapsed, bringing the material to within approximately five metres of the river. Water levels in the River Cherwell are climbing, increasing the risk of contamination.

The Environment Agency has indicated that its enforcement capabilities are constrained by limited resources, noting that the projected expense of removal exceeds the total yearly budget of the local district council.
