Government officials from federal and Victorian levels are intensifying support for families and primary producers impacted by January's bushfires in Victoria, building upon previous disaster recovery initiatives. Premier Jacinta Allan announced that uninsured homeowners who have lost their primary residence will be eligible for grants up to $52,250. This fresh assistance complements the existing Commonwealth–State Disaster Recovery Funding Arrangements and the initial aid provided by the Personal Hardship Assistance Program.

Emergency Management Minister Kristy McBain emphasized the Albanese government's collaborative efforts with Victoria during the recovery phase, assuring Victorians of unwavering support throughout both fire response and recovery efforts.

Assistance programs focus on providing farmers and livestock with necessary access. In response to substantial losses in agricultural zones, the Victorian government is enhancing mental health and business aid for farmers. The Rural Financial Counselling Service will provide complimentary, confidential wellbeing assistance, coupled with financial and business counselling. The “Look Over the Farm Gate” initiative, managed by the National Centre for Farmer Health, is scheduled to commence on Jan. 16, offering grants of up to $5,000 for local events and activities in farming areas to support mental health and community recovery.

Animal welfare remains a pressing issue for farmers unable to safely revisit their lands. Property owners may submit requests to Agriculture Victoria, which collaborates with the Fire Incident Controller to determine safe access for monitoring animals.

The Victorian government has launched the 2026 Victorian Bushfire Appeal to facilitate public donations. Minister Vicki Ward stated that the appeal aims to distribute funds based on priorities set by local communities, with the state covering administrative expenses.

Simultaneously, insurance providers are noting an uptick in claims volumes. The Insurance Council of Australia reported 1,386 claims since January 7, spanning home, commercial, motor, and business interruption policies. Insurers have initiated catastrophe protocols, processing claims and dispatching personnel to affected regions as access improves. IAG established a mobile claims unit in Seymour to assist patrons, while Suncorp activated its extreme weather protocol, registering approximately 60 claims related to the Victorian bushfires by January 11.

Health funds are revising policies for clients in disaster-stricken areas. HCF has rolled out a relief initiative offering eligible members premium waivers of up to three months, the ability to pause coverage for up to two years, and access to the HealthyMinds Check-in program for psychological support. Additionally, HCF is providing complimentary online GP consultations through its collaboration with GP2U.
