data.fused_content:
Colombo: Sri Lanka announced a state of emergency on Saturday following heavy rains and floods that have increased the fatality count to 132, with an additional 176 individuals reported missing.

According to the Disaster Management Centre (DMC), over 15,000 homes have been devastated by the severe weather conditions, forcing 78,000 people to seek refuge in government-managed temporary shelters.

In response to the widespread destruction caused by a week of heavy rainfall throughout the island, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake enacted emergency legislation, affording him extensive authority to address the crisis.

"We have 132 confirmed fatalities and an additional 176 individuals unaccounted for," stated Sampath Kotuwegoda, Director-General of the DMC, noting that the reinforcement of relief efforts had been achieved through the involvement of the army, navy, and air force.

The military evacuated 69 bus passengers, including a German tourist, on Saturday from the Anuradhapura district following a 24-hour mission. A passenger recounted at a local hospital how navy sailors assisted them in reaching the roof of a neighboring house, employing ropes to navigate through the floodwaters safely.

"Fortune was on our side as we stood on the roof, a section gave way," said Shantha, a passenger. "Three women ended up in the water, yet they were assisted back up to the roof."

A helicopter was forced to abandon its first rescue mission because the rotor's downdraft posed a risk of dislodging the roof where they were perched. Eventually, naval boats arrived to rescue them.

The roads throughout Badulla's central district continued to be impassable, isolating numerous villages and preventing the delivery of relief supplies. "Our village mourns the loss of two residents," expressed Saman Kumara from Maspanna village in Badulla, one of the worst-affected districts. "Our village is completely isolated; earth slips have obstructed all roads, preventing anyone from leaving or entering. We're facing a scarcity of food and have limited clean water supplies."

According to officials, approximately one-third of the nation lacked electricity and access to running water due to the collapse of power lines and the flooding of water purification plants. Internet connectivity also remained compromised in numerous regions.

On Saturday, Cyclone Ditwah departed from the island, charting a course northward towards India's shores. Chennai Airport in India has grounded 54 flights due to the impending cyclone, as meteorologists predict severe downpours and gusty winds for the upcoming 48 hours.

The central district of Kandy, situated 115 kilometres (71 miles) east of Colombo, has been recently struck by landslides, causing the main access road to become submerged in several areas.

The government has issued an appeal to the international community for help and urged Sri Lankans living overseas to contribute financially to aid the impacted communities. According to officials, Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya held meetings with diplomats stationed in Colombo to brief them on the current circumstances and request support from their respective governments.

India was the first to respond by dispatching two aircraft filled with essential relief supplies. As part of a pre-scheduled goodwill mission, an Indian warship docked in Colombo offered its provisions to help victims. Prime Minister Narendra Modi of India conveyed his sympathy for the fatalities in Sri Lanka, affirming that New Delhi was prepared to provide additional assistance.

Flooding in low-lying areas worsened on Saturday, prompting authorities to issue evacuation orders for those living along the banks of the Kelani River, which empties into the Indian Ocean from Colombo.

The rain had subsided across much of the nation, including the capital, but the northern part of the island continued to experience rainfall as a lingering consequence of Cyclone Ditwah.

Since 2017, the cyclone stands as the most lethal natural calamity to strike Sri Lanka. In June 2003, Sri Lanka faced its most severe flooding since the start of the century, resulting in the deaths of 254 individuals and forcing hundreds of thousands to seek refuge elsewhere.
