Typhoon Kalmaegi, the latest in a series of late-season storms to impact the Philippines in recent years, has carved a path of destruction through the central Visayas region this week. The storm aligns with a concerning trend of intense tropical cyclones that have struck the nation over the last 15 years, especially during the months of October to December.

Analysts attribute the increasing frequency of these severe weather incidents to climate change, accelerated urbanization, and inadequate disaster preparedness. Ven Paolo Valenzuela, a research fellow at the College of Integrative Studies at Singapore Management University, told This Week in Asia, "For over ten years, we have persistently ignored the wake-up call to enhance disaster preparedness, repeatedly hitting the snooze button."

Arriving in Philippine territory on Sunday as a tropical storm, Kalmaegi—referred to locally as Typhoon Tino—rapidly gained strength within mere hours. The fierce winds ripped roofs off houses, uprooted trees and power lines, and carried away vehicles and temporary shelters.
