COLOMBO, Dec. 9 (Xinhua) — Humanitarian flood relief supplies from the Chinese government reached Colombo on Monday morning and were officially transferred to Sri Lankan authorities.

Chinese Ambassador to Sri Lanka Qi Zhenhong, Sri Lanka's Minister of Ports and Civil Aviation Anura Karunathilake, and Deputy Minister of Defense Aruna Jayasekera attended the handover ceremony.

Karunathilake conveyed heartfelt appreciation for China's prompt and generous aid, highlighting that the supplies delivered meet the critical needs of Sri Lanka. He emphasized that China has consistently offered assistance to Sri Lanka during challenging times throughout the years. Amid the current crisis, he noted that the Chinese government, businesses, and citizens residing in Sri Lanka have rendered substantial aid.

He expressed gratitude towards the Chinese government and its people for their selfless support and reiterated his hope for the lasting friendship between Sri Lanka and China.

Qi mentioned that in response to this serious natural disaster, all pertinent Chinese agencies promptly took action to offer assistance to their Sri Lankan counterparts. He observed that both Chinese enterprises and the Chinese diaspora in Sri Lanka have proactively engaged in aiding the local disaster relief initiatives. China remains committed to extending support to Sri Lanka to the best of its abilities, as it has consistently done in the past.

The Chinese government provided humanitarian aid to Sri Lanka, consisting of essential supplies such as tents, bed sheets, life jackets, and blankets.

Sri Lanka's Disaster Management Center reported that Cyclone Ditwah unleashed devastating weather conditions across the nation, resulting in the tragic loss of over 600 lives, demolishing over 4,000 homes and inflicting partial damage on upwards of 60,000 residences, impacting over 2 million residents.
