In response to frigid temperatures and fierce winds, emergency shelters have been established in Victoria and Saanich, triggering an Extreme Weather Response throughout the area.

In Saanich, Broadview United Church has launched an Extreme Weather Response shelter, offering 20 overnight beds managed by Our Place Society with financial backing from the District of Saanich provided by BC Housing. Additionally, residents have the option to utilize shelter services provided by the Victoria Native Friendship Centre, which accommodates 50 individuals. The spaces will be accessible as long as severe weather conditions satisfy provincial activation standards, which may encompass freezing temperatures, prolonged rain, or snowfall. These EWR beds are distinct from regular nightly shelter accommodations and are deployed specifically when severe weather warnings arise.

Alongside provisional sleeping arrangements, Saanich has made daytime public warming facilities available. Residents are welcome to keep warm at municipal recreation centers—including Saanich Commonwealth Place, Gordon Head Recreation Centre, Cedar Hill Recreation and Arts Centre, and G.R. Pearkes Recreation Centre—as well as the Saanich municipal hall during standard hours of operation. Operational hours may fluctuate during severe weather due to staffing availability.

Environment Canada predicts strong winds increasing to 30 kilometres per hour, with gusts possibly reaching 70 kilometres per hour near the Juan de Fuca Strait, alongside temperatures approaching freezing. Overnight temperatures are expected to fall to approximately 1°C, with chillier conditions inland.

In Victoria, the municipality reports the addition of 30 more shelter mats under the Emergency Weather Response initiative, provided by collaborators at the Salvation Army ARC. Overnight accommodations are accessible at 525 Johnson St beginning at 8 p.m. on Friday and concluding at 7 a.m. on Saturday. The facility accommodates individuals of all genders and has a strict no-pet policy.
