The death toll from floods and landslides on Sumatra has risen to 174, with approximately 80 individuals still missing, according to disaster officials. The National Disaster Management Agency reported that torrential monsoon rains caused rivers in North Sumatra province to overflow earlier this week, sweeping through mountainside villages and affecting more than 3,200 homes and structures.

National Disaster Mitigation Agency chief Suharyanto confirmed that fatalities include 116 in North Sumatra, 25 in Aceh, and 23 in West Sumatra. Thousands of homes in Aceh and West Sumatra were inundated, with many submerged up to their roofs. In West Sumatra alone, over 17,000 homes were flooded, compelling around 23,000 people to seek refuge in temporary shelters.

The floods and landslides destroyed rice fields, livestock, and public facilities while severing bridges and roads, isolating residents. The extreme weather was driven by tropical cyclone Senyar, which formed in the Strait of Malacca, causing mudslides and rockfalls onto hilly settlements.

Achadi Subarkah Raharjo from Indonesia’s Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysical Agency explained that the extreme weather warning has been extended due to robust water vapor supply and shifting atmospheric dynamics. He cautioned that ongoing unstable conditions could prolong the severe weather while the cyclone system remains active. The persistent downpours have rendered the steep, saturated terrain extremely susceptible to disasters.

In Indonesia, an archipelago of 17,000 islands, seasonal rains frequently trigger flooding and landslides, affecting millions residing in mountainous regions or near fertile flood plains.
