data.fused_content:
COLOMBO - Sri Lanka has declared a state of emergency and is seeking global aid as fatalities from floods and landslides induced by Cyclone Ditwah climbed to 153, with 191 individuals unaccounted for. The severe weather conditions have devastated more than 20,000 homes, forcing 108,000 people into government-run temporary shelters, while 798,000 others require aid after being displaced.

A passenger, recounting from the hospital, described how navy sailors used ropes to guide them safely to the roof of a nearby house above the floodwaters. An initial helicopter rescue attempt was halted when the rotor downdraft risked dislodging the roof; subsequent rescue was completed by naval boats.

In the central district of Badulla, roads remain impassable, isolating several villages and blocking relief supplies. About a third of the country was without electricity or running water after power lines collapsed and water purification facilities were inundated. Saman Kumara from Maspanna, one of the hardest-hit areas in Badulla, reported via telephone that two people were lost in his village, with others taking refuge in a temple and one remaining house. "We can't leave the village, and no one can enter as all routes are obstructed by landslides. We lack food and are running out of clean water," he said.

Clean water was scarce, and internet connections were disrupted. Cyclone Ditwah departed Sri Lanka on Saturday, heading towards India. Chennai Airport in India cancelled 54 flights due to the cyclone, with forecasts predicting intense rain and high winds for the next 48 hours.

The Sri Lankan government has called for international assistance and encouraged Sri Lankans overseas to contribute financially to support impacted communities. An Indian warship, visiting Colombo on a pre-scheduled goodwill mission, donated its provisions to aid the victims. Personnel from the army, navy, and air force, along with civilian workers and volunteers, have been mobilized to support relief operations.
