Severe weather has battered North Sumatra for several consecutive days, leading to flooding in parts of the Tapanuli Selatan district since Monday. The calamity of floods and landslides has claimed the lives of eight individuals, resulted in 58 injuries, and led to the evacuation of 2,851 residents.

The local disaster agency has dispatched heavy machinery to remove debris obstructing road access to the district. At least three other districts within the province have similarly experienced the impact of flooding and landslides.

According to the Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency, the extreme weather is attributed to cyclone seeds, which are weather formations capable of developing into tropical cyclones. The yearly monsoon period, generally spanning from June to September, frequently results in intense rainfall, setting off landslides and flash floods, and the spread of water-borne diseases.

Alterations in storm patterns due to climate change have affected both the length and strength of the season, resulting in increased rainfall, more frequent flash floods, and intensified wind gusts. This month, landslides caused by intense rainfall have claimed the lives of at least 38 individuals, with approximately 13 others still unaccounted for.
