Unrelenting extreme weather has battered North Sumatra for several consecutive days, inundating portions of the Tapanuli Selatan district since Monday, as reported by the national disaster agency, BNPB.

In Tapanuli Selatan, the calamity of floods and landslides has led to the tragic loss of eight lives, injuries to 58 individuals, and the displacement of 2,851 residents who were forced to evacuate. Approximately 13 individuals remain unaccounted for.

Abdul Muhari, spokesperson for BNPB, stated that the local disaster agency has dispatched heavy machinery to remove debris obstructing road access to the district. He mentioned that flooding and landslides have impacted at least three additional districts within the province.

According to the Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency, the extreme weather conditions are attributed to cyclone seeds, which are weather formations with the capacity to develop into tropical cyclones.

The yearly monsoon period, usually spanning from June to September, frequently results in intense rainfall, which can cause landslides, flash flooding, and the spread of waterborne illnesses. The effects of climate change have altered storm patterns, modifying the length and intensity of the season, resulting in increased rainfall, more frequent flash floods, and intensified wind gusts.

This month, landslides caused by intense rainfall in Central Java claimed the lives of at least 38 individuals.
