A lightning flash extending 515 miles from Texas to Missouri has been recognized as the longest electrical bolt in the world, surpassing the previous record by approximately 38 miles. This finding emerged from an analysis of satellite imagery by the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration's GOES-16 series, which captured the event on October 22, 2017.

The World Meteorological Organization confirmed the new benchmark, noting that these occurrences are classified as "megaflashes"—extremely long duration and distance lightning discharge events. The bolt originated within a significant thunderstorm system in a recognized "storm hotspot" in the Great Plains area of the United States.

Professor Randall Cerveny of the WMO's Weather and Climate Extremes highlighted that advances in science and technology now allow for the evaluation of such extreme weather phenomena. "It is likely that even greater extremes still exist," Cerveny remarked, adding that improved measurements will enable further observations over time.

The GOES-16 satellite, part of the Geostationary Operational Environmental Satellite program launched in 2016, documented images of megaflashes in both 2017 and 2020. The GOES-R series, which includes four satellites, was completed with its final launch in June 2024.

NOAA and NASA collaborate on monitoring and researching lightning activity to enhance weather prediction and comprehension. The United States experiences approximately 25 million cloud-to-ground lightning strikes annually, associated with significant fatalities, injuries, and financial losses. The National Centers for Environmental Information reported that 27 significant natural disasters in 2024—including severe storms, tropical cyclones, and winter events—collectively incurred costs of $182.7 billion.

Meteorological forecasts anticipate intensified weather phenomena, rising temperatures, and shifting climate patterns leading to stronger storms. Financial impacts from natural disasters are expected to increase further in the coming years.
