Alicante is poised to face severe weather conditions precisely one year after catastrophic floods claimed 220 lives in the region. Travelers from the UK planning a visit to the Costa Blanca have been cautioned following an uncommon weather advisory for heavy rain issued for the holiday hotspot, which experienced lethal flooding the previous year.

A rare red alert has been issued for Spain's southeastern coast, escalating warnings released earlier this week and putting the region on high alert due to impending "extraordinary danger." The measure will begin at 10 am local time on Friday.

AEMET, Spain’s meteorological authority, issued a warning on Thursday of potential significant and continuous rainfall through Monday in Alicante. The forecaster stated that intense showers could lead to localized flash flooding in low-lying regions, streams, and gullies, significantly elevating the risk level.

On Wednesday, significant portions of the Costa Blanca, encompassing well-known tourist spots such as Alicante, Benidorm, and Valencia, were issued yellow alerts. On Friday, AEMET escalated the warning to a red alert for intense rainfall covering the entirety of Alicante and portions of Murcia. Valencia's advisory has been raised to amber, anticipating up to 140 mm of rain within the forthcoming 12 hours.

The UK Foreign Office has advised British travelers heading to Spain to expect severe weather conditions and possible travel disruptions. They announced that today’s severe weather alerts affect the regions of Murcia and Valencia, encompassing Alicante province, and urged travelers to consult communications from airlines or travel providers before embarking on their journey. Those in impacted regions were advised to adhere to guidance from local officials and monitor regional weather reports.

The tempest arrives a year after the October 2024 calamity, during which Spain contended with a lethal weather emergency. Survivors compared the scenes to a post-apocalyptic survival depicted in the film The Road. Brit Jerome Torres, aged 49, recounted the terrifying experience of listening to his neighbors' last cries as devastating floods swept through, revealing he heard their screams just before they perished. Torres remarked that the Spanish city resembled a scene from a "horror film," having been ravaged by torrential flooding that left homes and businesses in ruins and streets blanketed in mud.

It marked the most devastating flood disaster in Europe since 1967, a year when Portugal saw the tragic loss of at least 500 lives.
