language_errors.fused_text:
Mongolia faces a severe climatic phenomenon known locally as the "white death" or dzud, characterized by summer drought followed by an intensely harsh winter. Temperatures can plunge to below -30 degrees Celsius, with heavy snowfall enveloping the landscape. Although traditionally occurring every few years, climate change has altered their frequency, leading some areas to experience dzuds annually.

The winter of 2022-2023 was exceptionally harsh, with 90% of Mongolia's land covered in thick snow, marking the heaviest snowfall in 49 years. Millions of livestock perished not only from the extreme cold but also from malnutrition, as droughts during the summer months had severely limited food cultivation. Tragically, the nation’s herding communities bear the brunt of this impact, as the loss of livestock often equates to a loss of livelihood for families reliant on selling their produce. According to the World Health Organization, 5.9 million people were affected in 2023, and 9% of the country's entire herd was lost.

As the snow eventually melts, widespread flooding occurs, further devastating infrastructure and grazing areas while heightening the risk of food and waterborne illnesses, especially in impoverished regions with rudimentary sanitation systems. Thus, the dzud manifests as both a natural calamity and a socio-economic crisis.

Ongoing intervention efforts involve multiple organizations. The WHO and World Bank have collaborated with the Mongolian Ministry of Health to ensure rural populations receive necessary aid. Frontline health workers are providing medical supplies and psychological support in 21 affected provinces. The World Bank has also partnered with meteorological and environmental agencies to develop a comprehensive disaster risk financing strategy. Weather data is used to trigger preemptive funding releases, ensuring herding families receive financial assistance before the dzud begins. Additionally, the Mongolian Red Cross Society has been instrumental in helping herder households recover, delivering critical humanitarian assistance and psychological support to the hardest-hit families.

These efforts demonstrate that early intervention, social protection programs, and community support can make a significant difference in mitigating the dzud's devastating effects.
