data.fused_content:
Heavy rainfall has inundated southern and central Taiwan over the past week, causing floods, landslides, and road closures, severely damaging properties and prompting evacuations. The downpours forced school and office closures in some regions. Agricultural losses exceeded NT$2.6 billion (US$86.6 million), with over 23,000 roofs damaged and nearly 2,500 utility poles toppled, resulting in widespread power failures.

During last month’s mass recall campaigns, which coincided with a typhoon, several lawmakers from the Chinese Nationalist Party (KMT) exploited the timing to rally support against the recalls. The KMT shared an image of Tainan Mayor Huang Wei-che of the Democratic Progressive Party (DPP) attending a recall event in June, accusing him of prioritizing recalls over disaster relief—though the typhoon had not yet developed when the photo was taken. Taichung Mayor Lu Shiow-yen of the KMT asserted that DPP lawmakers were solely preoccupied with the campaigns, despite visits to affected areas by the president, premier, and local leaders. Chiayi Mayor Huang Min-hui of the KMT thanked Kaohsiung Mayor Chen Chi-mai of the DPP for swiftly dispatching personnel to assist with relief efforts in Chiayi City. Meanwhile, KMT Legislator Niu Hsu-ting criticized the sharing of photos depicting Taiwan Power Co workers repairing utility poles post-typhoon as "emotional blackmail" of voters, noting that KMT lawmakers had previously reduced a subsidy for the utility.

The Central Weather Administration (CWA) shared a chart on Facebook detailing the intensity of the brief but heavy rainfall, linking hourly statistics to common perceptions to aid public comprehension. Rainfall exceeding 80mm per hour was characterized as “creating a sensation akin to pressure or suffocation, as though the sky is falling; surpassing most urban drainage systems' capacities; and readily triggering floods and landslides.” Several weather stations reported rainfall surpassing 100mm per hour, with Sandimen Township in Pingtung County recording 122mm per hour. The CWA’s initiative to communicate scientific facts in clear language exemplifies impartial public outreach.

The government must address misinformation earnestly, devising clear methods to communicate facts and policies effectively, not only to counter opposition narratives but also to restore public confidence and fortify resilience.
