A Senate inquiry into a major algal bloom off the coast of South Australia has been approved with bipartisan support from Labor, the coalition, and the Greens. The bloom, first detected near the Fleurieu Peninsula in March, has resulted in the deaths of tens of thousands of marine creatures and has spread along the coastline.

The ecological catastrophe has prompted a collaborative $28 million investment package co-financed by the South Australian and federal governments. The funding is directed toward clean-up, recovery, research, and industry assistance. On Tuesday, Premier Peter Malinauskas confirmed the SA government would provide a matching $14 million commitment.

Nationals senator Ross Cadell remarked that the widespread backing indicated the matter transcended political boundaries. “Whether it be flood, drought, bushfire, or algal bloom, our communities need support, not politics,” Cadell said. He added that the investigation offers an opportunity to understand the tangible consequences of the event and evaluate the suitability of the existing disaster relief funding framework.

The algal bloom has been described as unprecedented, leaving uncertainty about its duration and additional consequences. Residents have reported murky groundwater, a dense layer of foam on typically stunning beaches, and dead fish, dolphins, rays, and sharks washing ashore.

As part of the response package, $8.5 million is allocated to a coastal science and research network designed to enhance early detection and monitoring of harmful algal bloom species. A $3 million rapid assessment will evaluate the effects on fish stocks and fisheries. Additionally, a newly established $2 million national testing lab in South Australia will eliminate the necessity of sending samples to New Zealand for analysis.
