This spring, Türkiye's lemon orchards experienced severe damage due to frost, hail, and extreme heat. Farmers in the southern Adana region reported significant losses, with one citrus grower noting nearly 40 percent of their produce was destroyed by these three calamities.

The Çukurova Valley, a pivotal agricultural hub responsible for approximately 40 percent of Türkiye's citrus production, has been particularly affected. Mehmet Akın Doğan, president of the Yüreğir Chamber of Agriculture, stated that climate change impacts are increasingly jeopardizing farming operations in this fertile region.

Similar to other Mediterranean nations, Türkiye has seen a rise in extreme weather incidents in recent years, threatening rural agricultural communities. The adverse conditions have extended beyond citrus crops, affecting other produce including apricots.

Orhan Karaca, an apricot farmer with four decades of experience, remarked he had never encountered such devastation. He noted that farmers are now struggling not just to preserve their crops, but their very trees, characterizing the frost's impact as particularly severe. Karaca, who leads the agricultural chamber in Malatya, compared the frost's devastation to being "more severe for us than the February 2023 earthquake" in some affected regions.
