A report has revealed that since 2020, the UK has lost a year's supply of bread due to harvests being devastated by severe weather conditions. Specialists at the Energy and Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU) estimated that this quantity of wheat could produce over 4 billion loaves of bread, equating to a year's worth of supply.

Extreme weather events, intensified by climate change, have resulted in a shortfall of more than 7 million tonnes in wheat output. Confronted with unprecedented heat and drought conditions, alongside restrictions on irrigation, farmers faced significant challenges in sowing their crops. This year, crops endured severe challenges due to one of the hottest and driest springs and summers ever recorded.

This decade has witnessed two of the top three poorest harvests in history, with expectations of further deterioration. The most severe year was 2020, followed by the third worst in 2024. The situation is anticipated to worsen as climate change induces extended spells of drought alongside intensified, severe rainfall, potentially leading to floods.

The climate crisis introduces unpredictability for farmers, causing various growers to face alternating periods of prosperity and decline. Tom Lancaster, an analyst specializing in land, food, and farming at the ECIU, commented: “This is the reality of farming amid a shifting climate. Adverse weather conditions are diminishing the British nature of our bread, as millers increasingly rely on imports to compensate for reduced UK production.” The UK's wheat self-sufficiency decreased from 96% in 2023 to 79% in 2024. Last autumn, wheat imports were twice the five-year average.

Global wheat shortages are intensifying supply challenges internationally. Various countries face their own issues, as conflicts and adverse weather conditions are reducing their crop outputs.

Colin Chappell, an arable farmer from Lincolnshire, said: “I have never known a more challenging time to farm. As a farmer, I am used to managing the worst the weather has to throw at me, but recent years have become impossible. Extended periods of heavy rainfall are succeeded by relentless droughts, with no respite in between, leaving me staring down the barrel of a second consecutive disastrous harvest. I require additional assistance to adapt to these extremes and build the resilience of my business. However, the closure of green farming initiatives in England means that such assistance is unavailable to me. Reopening these programs must be prioritized to ensure farmers can keep cultivating wheat and other essential crops for the British populace.”

The drought experienced this year suggests that 2025 will likely face yet another disappointing harvest.
