TUOLUMNE COUNTY, Calif. (AP) — A rapidly advancing wildfire has destroyed homes in a historic California Gold Rush town established around 1850 by Chinese miners who were expelled from a neighboring camp.

The blaze, part of the TCU September Lightning Complex, swiftly grew to encompass over 19 square miles across various areas in Calaveras and Tuolumne counties, with no containment as of early Wednesday. It prompted the evacuation of Chinese Camp and nearby roads on Tuesday, according to CalFire, the state's primary firefighting agency.

In the rural town, at least five homes were engulfed in flames, comprising a blend of standalone houses and mobile residences. A recreational vehicle on the premises also sustained damage. On Tuesday evening, outside a residence, seven individuals cleared large tree branches and tossed sand onto the flames in an effort to prevent the fire from spreading, continuing their efforts for approximately half an hour until firefighters arrived.

Fire officials have called for additional assets, including fire engines, bulldozers, and aircraft. CalFire identified lightning as the cause of the fire, referred to as the 6-5 Fire, and noted it is among over a dozen fires that ignited across California on Tuesday.

Amid the Gold Rush, thousands of Chinese immigrants arrived in California, where they encountered significant persecution, including an excessive Foreign Miners Tax aimed at forcing them out of mining. Chinese miners established the settlement after being expelled from a nearby camp; initially named Camp Washington, it was later renamed to acknowledge the influx of Chinese settlers.
