Climate change has increased the likelihood of extreme weather conditions that triggered severe wildfires across Spain and Portugal last month by a factor of 40, according to preliminary analysis from the World Weather Attribution network. The fires, which ravaged 500,000 hectares (1.2 million acres) of land in just weeks, were also 30% more intense than scientists would anticipate in a scenario unaffected by climate change.

Researchers noted that the magnitude of these fires was "truly remarkable." With climate change intensifying, conditions that are hotter, drier, and more prone to ignition are increasingly severe, leading to fires of unmatched ferocity. The scientists determined that these circumstances might have arisen once every 500 years during the preindustrial era but are now likely to occur every 15 years.

The impact of climate breakdown on extreme heat was even more pronounced. Previously, the 10-day peak temperatures observed in the region last month were predicted to occur once every 2,500 years before industrialization. Now, they are anticipated to happen every 13 years.

The research, which has yet to undergo peer review, relied on meteorological data instead of the typical climate model assessments that the team usually performs right after severe weather events. A separate comprehensive study released last week on wildfires in Turkey and Greece revealed that climate disruption had increased the likelihood of such extreme weather conditions by a factor of 10.

Alterations in land utilization have exacerbated the escalating threat of uncontrolled fires in the region. The migration of young individuals to urban areas has resulted in neglected farmland, now overrun with dense, highly flammable vegetation. David Garcia, an applied mathematician at the University of Alicante, noted that discussions in Spain have centered on diminishing rural activities and the consequent expansion of vegetation.

Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez unveiled a 10-point climate agenda on Monday during an interview with the Guardian. The comprehensive initiative is designed to address the growing severity of extreme weather conditions. Sánchez cautioned that mainstream right-wing parties were jeopardizing climate initiatives by embracing far-right rhetoric. He stated, "The issue confronting us today is that conventional right-wing parties might not refute the scientific truth, yet they conduct themselves as though climate change is not a reality." He emphasized that this attitude represents the biggest problem in advancing climate action.
