Hurricane Beryl, the first major hurricane of the 2024 Atlantic season, set a record as the earliest Category 5 hurricane on record. Passing approximately 150 km south of Barbados, it struck the island as a Category 3 storm, generating large swells and waves that impacted the southern and western coasts.

While the hurricane caused only moderate damage to core infrastructure and did not trigger a humanitarian crisis, coastal assets suffered substantial harm. The total impact was valued at 193 million Barbados dollars, equivalent to 0.15% of the nation's GDP. Damage accounted for 58% of this impact, followed by losses at 36%, with additional expenses making up the remaining 5%. The tourism, fisheries, agriculture, and environment sectors collectively represented 84% of these effects.

This early-season storm underscores the increasing unpredictability of climate patterns and highlights the vulnerability of Caribbean small island developing states to extreme weather events. It emphasizes the ongoing need to strengthen all phases of disaster risk management—including risk assessment, prevention, mitigation, preparedness, and response—to enable nations and communities to effectively confront challenges posed by severe weather and climate change.
