North Sumatra has been battered by severe weather for several days, leading to flooding in areas of the Tapanuli Selatan district since Monday, as reported by the national disaster agency BNPB.

"In Tapanuli Selatan, the calamity of floods and landslides has led to the tragic loss of eight lives, injuries to 58 individuals, and forced 2,851 residents to evacuate their homes," stated Abdul Muhari, a spokesman for BNPB.

According to him, the local disaster agency has sent heavy machinery to remove debris that has obstructed the roads leading to the district. He noted that flooding and landslides have impacted at least three other districts within the province as well.

The Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency attributes the severe weather to cyclone seeds, which are weather formations with the capacity to develop into tropical cyclones.

The monsoon period, spanning from June to September, is frequently characterized by substantial rainfall, frequently results in torrential downpours, causing landslides, flash floods, and the spread of waterborne diseases.

The alteration in climate has influenced storm dynamics, affecting both the length and severity of the season, which results in intensified rainfall, sudden floods, and more powerful wind bursts.

This month, landslides caused by intense rainfall in Central Java have resulted in the deaths of at least 38 individuals, with approximately 13 people still unaccounted for.
