data.fused_content:
A report has revealed that since 2020, the UK has lost an entire year's supply of bread due to extreme weather conditions devastating crop harvests. Climate breakdown has intensified droughts and floods, leading to a shortfall in wheat production exceeding 7 million tonnes. According to the Energy and Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU), this deficit equates to more than 4 billion loaves of bread – enough for a year's provision.

Faced with unprecedented heat, drought conditions, and restrictions on water use, farmers found it challenging to sow their crops. This decade has seen two of the three poorest harvests ever recorded, with 2020 being the worst and 2024 the third worst. Excessive rainfall thwarted farmers' efforts to plant and manage crops, while this year presented a different struggle with one of the hottest and driest springs and summers documented.

With drought conditions persisting, 2025 is anticipated to be another challenging year for crop yields. The situation is expected to deteriorate further as climate change leads to extended dry spells combined with more intense rainfall, potentially resulting in floods. Meanwhile, the abundance of sunny days is projected to make 2025 a vintage year for wine production, and apple orchards have yielded an abundant harvest.

The climate emergency is generating unpredictability for farmers, as various growers encounter alternating periods of prosperity and hardship. Tom Lancaster, an analyst specializing in land, food, and farming for the ECIU, commented: "This exemplifies the reality of farming amidst climate change. Unpredictable weather patterns are rendering our bread less British, costing British farmers billions in lost income and reducing our self-sufficiency in our main staple crop." The UK's wheat self-sufficiency decreased from 96% in 2023 to 79% in 2024.

To produce flour for bread, cakes, and biscuits, millers are bringing in unprecedented quantities of wheat. Last autumn, wheat imports surged to twice the five-year average. The scarcity of wheat is straining international supplies, as other nations face their own challenges with conflicts and severe weather conditions reducing crop yields.

Colin Chappell, a crop farmer from Lincolnshire, expressed: "Farming has never been more difficult. While I'm accustomed to handling the harshest weather conditions, the recent years have turned unmanageable. Extended periods of rain are succeeded by stretches of drought, appearing relentless, and I am now confronting a second consecutive disastrous harvest. I require additional assistance to adjust to these extreme conditions and fortify my business's resilience. However, with the green farming schemes in England currently closed, that support isn't available to me. Reopening them must be prioritized if farmers are to continue growing wheat and other key crops for the Great British public."
