data.fused_content:
A rapidly advancing wildfire consumed homes in a California Gold Rush town on Wednesday, continuing to expand unchecked. The blaze, part of the TCU September Lightning Complex, swiftly grew to cover an area of 10 square miles, prompting the evacuation of Chinese Camp and nearby highways on Tuesday.

There were no immediate reports of injuries or fatalities. The fire, which CalFire said was ignited by lightning, is among over a dozen that ignited across California on Tuesday. By Wednesday morning, containment efforts had not been successful in Calaveras and Tuolumne counties.

At least five residences were ablaze in the rural area, characterized by a combination of standalone and mobile homes. Tuesday evening saw a desperate attempt to keep the blaze from spreading from a neighboring house. A group of seven individuals hastily relocated large tree branches and shoveled sand onto the flames. Their efforts lasted approximately 30 minutes until firefighters arrived. An RV on the property was damaged by the fire.

Fire officials have called for more resources, including fire engines, bulldozers, and aircraft.

The town of Chinese Camp was established circa 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from an adjacent encampment. Initially named Camp Washington, it was later renamed to honor the influx of thousands of Chinese settlers. During the Gold Rush, Chinese immigrants endured widespread discrimination and persecution, including the imposition of a steep Foreign Miners Tax intended to force them out of the mining industry.
