The Environment Agency has completed 151 flood management projects across England over the past year, significantly enhancing protection for more than 24,000 homes and businesses. These initiatives, which include both new constructions and upgrades to existing defences, were finalized between July 2024 and July 2025 following Labour's rise to power.

Notable completed schemes include the Saltfleet to Gibraltar Point Beach Management project in Lincolnshire, which better protects 2,904 local properties; the Cockett Wick seawall improvement in Jaywick, safeguarding 1,526 properties; and the £75 million Canvey Island Flood Defence Scheme in Essex. The Canvey Island project renewed three kilometres of tidal defences, providing improved protection for over 6,000 properties for the next 50 years.

This progress aligns with the Government's commitment to increase flood defence spending, with £2.65 billion allocated over the next two years until April 2026—a £250 million increase over the previous Tory government's allocation. More broadly, the Government has pledged £7.9 billion for flood defences from 2026 to 2036, bringing the total investment to over £10.5 billion across the 12 years from 2024.

Caroline Douglass, executive director of floods and coastal risk management at the Environment Agency, emphasized the critical importance of these projects: "Flooding has a devastating impact on communities across the country and will continue to worsen as extreme weather is caused by our changing climate. Our teams and partners have worked diligently to deliver another 151 vitally needed flood schemes nationwide—ready to protect people and homes this winter."

The investment is expected to benefit more than 890,000 properties, protecting businesses, safeguarding farmland, and supporting the Government's plans to build 1.5 million new homes by 2029. Ministers say it will also drive economic growth, create jobs, and help towns and cities prosper for future generations.

The Environment Agency notes that 6.3 million properties in England are currently at risk of flooding, with this number projected to rise to around eight million—one in four properties—by 2050 due to rising sea levels, more volatile rainfall patterns, outdated defences, and increased development.
